{
  "requirement": "The `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents, when a change adds, alters, or removes no end-user-observable behavior, to tell the user that no requirement is needed, and when an existing requirement already specifies the behavior the change produces, to tell the user which requirement covers it; in either case it MUST direct them to proceed to implementation without drafting.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: The text sets no structural obligation. It names no library, algorithm, signature, type, field or delegation interface. Every clause says what the `habeas-requirements` skill must direct agents to do:\n- Tell the user that no requirement is needed when a change adds, alters or removes no end-user-observable behavior.\n- Tell the user which requirement covers the behavior when an existing requirement already specifies it.\n- In both cases, proceed to implementation without drafting.\n\nI considered whether any clause fixes how the skill is built. The skill could put these instructions in a different section or word them differently. It could also be reorganized, and it would still satisfy the text as long as it gives the directions. So the placement and wording are discretionary.\n\nThe supplied root, `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md`, does carry the directions. In `section[Habeas requirements]/section[Workflow]`, step 1 (`paragraph@e93afed4f34e`) says: \"If the change adds, alters, or removes no observable behavior ... or an existing requirement already specifies it ..., tell the user which requirement covers it, or that none is needed, and go to step 6.\" Step 6 (`paragraph@f64f45ad5c25`) is \"Implement and test\". Steps 2 (draft) to 5 (commit) are skipped. Step 1 therefore routes both cases past drafting to implementation.\n\nNo further implementation roots are needed. Whether the skill's behavior actually holds in practice is a matter for the behavioral and test criteria, not this one.\n\nbehavioral: The requirement `requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill.no_change` states one behavior for the `habeas-requirements` skill. It has two triggers and one shared outcome:\n- When a change adds, alters, or removes no end-user-observable behavior, the skill directs agents to tell the user that no requirement is needed.\n- When an existing requirement already specifies the behavior, the skill directs agents to tell the user which requirement covers it.\n- In either case, the skill directs agents to proceed to implementation without drafting.\n\n`tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_skips_drafting_when_no_requirement_changes]` reads the bundled `habeas-requirements` skill text through `step(SKILL, 1)` and checks it with `assert_text`, which asserts that each phrase is present.\n- **No observable behavior:** it asserts the text \"If the change adds, alters, or removes no observable behavior\". It also asserts \"tell the user which requirement covers it, or that none is needed\", which covers the \"none needed\" notice.\n- **Existing requirement:** it asserts \"or an existing requirement already specifies it (most bug fixes), tell the user which requirement covers it\", which covers the \"which requirement covers it\" notice.\n- **Proceed to implementation, skipping drafting:** it asserts \"and go to step 6\". It then asserts `step_of(SKILL, \"Implement and test\") == 6`, so the jump target really is the implementation step. Skipping from step 1 to step 6 bypasses the drafting steps in between.\n\nThe requirement is about what the skill text directs, so asserting on the skill text is the right form of test. The assertions would fail if the text dropped either trigger, dropped the user notification, or pointed to a wrong step. I found no unconstrained obligation and no missing scenario.",
