        "rationale": "The requirement `requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill.no_change` states one behavior for the `habeas-requirements` skill. It has two triggers and one shared outcome:\n- When a change adds, alters, or removes no end-user-observable behavior, the skill directs agents to tell the user that no requirement is needed.\n- When an existing requirement already specifies the behavior, the skill directs agents to tell the user which requirement covers it.\n- In either case, the skill directs agents to proceed to implementation without drafting.\n\n`tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_skips_drafting_when_no_requirement_changes]` reads the bundled `habeas-requirements` skill text through `step(SKILL, 1)` and checks it with `assert_text`, which asserts that each phrase is present.\n- **No observable behavior:** it asserts the text \"If the change adds, alters, or removes no observable behavior\". It also asserts \"tell the user which requirement covers it, or that none is needed\", which covers the \"none needed\" notice.\n- **Existing requirement:** it asserts \"or an existing requirement already specifies it (most bug fixes), tell the user which requirement covers it\", which covers the \"which requirement covers it\" notice.\n- **Proceed to implementation, skipping drafting:** it asserts \"and go to step 6\". It then asserts `step_of(SKILL, \"Implement and test\") == 6`, so the jump target really is the implementation step. Skipping from step 1 to step 6 bypasses the drafting steps in between.\n\nThe requirement is about what the skill text directs, so asserting on the skill text is the right form of test. The assertions would fail if the text dropped either trigger, dropped the user notification, or pointed to a wrong step. I found no unconstrained obligation and no missing scenario."
